{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791811921,"nonce":"KfRSjQid4S3bbL6PeufZ54vH4Jk=","title":"Sys.command drops its optional argument array","message":"Sys.command(\"python3\", [\"-c\", \"import sys; sys.exit(42)\"]) returns 42 under native Haxe 4.3.6 and 0 under the frozen v60 Rayzor binary. The mapping exposes only a one-argument haxe_sys_command; the runtime starts the command through sh without using args. compiler/src/compilation/externs.rs explicitly documents this missing argument.\n\nTestUnicode.setupClass invokes python3 gen_test_res.py using an argument array. The script never runs, even after adding the missing class hook and staging the script in an isolated probe. Creating the fixture data externally gets the test into actual Unicode I/O failures. Sys.command must distinguish null/omitted args (shell command) from an explicit array (properly quoted/direct arguments), propagate its exit status, and retain inherited stdin/stdout/stderr.\n\nEvidence: target/conformance-checkpoint/sys-command-probe/CommandArguments.hx and sys-command-v60-baseline.log; unicode-io-v60e.log.","files":null}]}